WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:02.640
Instructor: Next up, amending commits.

00:02.640 --> 00:04.770
Occasionally when you're working with git,

00:04.770 --> 00:06.600
you may create a new commit

00:06.600 --> 00:09.030
and then realize just a moment too late

00:09.030 --> 00:12.480
that you forgot to include a relevant file in the commit.

00:12.480 --> 00:15.090
Or another thing that can happen is you make a commit

00:15.090 --> 00:17.070
and then you read over the commit message

00:17.070 --> 00:20.310
and you realize too late that you have a typo.

00:20.310 --> 00:23.040
You wrote something wrong in that commit message.

00:23.040 --> 00:25.620
So there are a couple of ways of fixing those problems,

00:25.620 --> 00:28.320
but the easiest one, the one I'm showing in this video,

00:28.320 --> 00:29.910
is to amend the commit.

00:29.910 --> 00:33.210
So this allows us to edit, or to redo, to update

00:33.210 --> 00:35.130
just the previous commit.

00:35.130 --> 00:38.730
So this doesn't work if you made a mistake 10 commits ago.

00:38.730 --> 00:43.080
This is only if you made a mistake just one commit ago.

00:43.080 --> 00:44.730
So here's how it works.

00:44.730 --> 00:45.990
When you make a commit,

00:45.990 --> 00:49.200
here on my slides I'm doing commit -m.

00:49.200 --> 00:50.280
I made some commit,

00:50.280 --> 00:53.400
then I realize, shoot, I forgot to include a file.

00:53.400 --> 00:55.890
So I add that file, I stage it,

00:55.890 --> 00:59.940
and then I get commit --amend

00:59.940 --> 01:03.090
and amend is going to redo the previous commit.

01:03.090 --> 01:04.530
You can read about it on the docs,

01:04.530 --> 01:08.130
although as always it's a little bit intimidating.

01:08.130 --> 01:09.720
I'll search for it on here.

01:09.720 --> 01:10.553
Amend,

01:11.460 --> 01:12.780
replace the tip of the current branch

01:12.780 --> 01:14.220
by creating a new commit.

01:14.220 --> 01:17.670
We haven't talked about branches, but creating a new commit.

01:17.670 --> 01:19.230
It's prepared as usual.

01:19.230 --> 01:20.610
The message from the original commit

01:20.610 --> 01:23.970
is used as the starting point instead of an empty message.

01:23.970 --> 01:25.590
I don't know if that's at all helpful to read.

01:25.590 --> 01:27.690
I'm just gonna show it to you.

01:27.690 --> 01:31.260
So let's go ahead and make a mistake.

01:31.260 --> 01:35.310
I'm going to add a little heading to every file.

01:35.310 --> 01:36.143
So at the top,

01:36.143 --> 01:39.630
I just want it to say outline, just as an example,

01:39.630 --> 01:43.110
and then I'll add some space in there, something like this.

01:43.110 --> 01:45.030
I'm gonna do that for all my other files.

01:45.030 --> 01:47.280
Chapter one, two, and characters.

01:47.280 --> 01:49.203
So let's do it for characters in here.

01:51.870 --> 01:54.120
So there I have a change in this file.

01:54.120 --> 01:56.343
Here's chapter one, or chapter two rather.

01:58.290 --> 02:01.680
So that's chapter two and then chapter one.

02:01.680 --> 02:03.630
Okay, so I've made those changes,

02:03.630 --> 02:06.360
just added a little heading to each one of those files.

02:06.360 --> 02:07.800
If I type git status,

02:07.800 --> 02:10.860
of course we see we modified four files.

02:10.860 --> 02:14.220
So let's go ahead and stage some of these files.

02:14.220 --> 02:16.323
Let's do chapter one.

02:18.750 --> 02:20.220
Let's do chapter two.

02:20.220 --> 02:23.970
I'll make this full screen and let's do the characters file,

02:23.970 --> 02:25.950
but I'm going to deliberately

02:25.950 --> 02:28.440
pretend to forget the outline file.

02:28.440 --> 02:30.720
So I'll add those three files.

02:30.720 --> 02:31.990
They should be staged

02:33.060 --> 02:36.330
and normally, I would check like I just did with git status

02:36.330 --> 02:38.370
and realize, oh, I forgot that file,

02:38.370 --> 02:41.400
but let's say I didn't do that and I commit.

02:41.400 --> 02:42.600
So git commit.

02:42.600 --> 02:45.240
I'll just do a -m commit,

02:45.240 --> 02:49.350
add heading to all files,

02:49.350 --> 02:54.330
or add headings to all files like that.

02:54.330 --> 02:59.330
Alrighty and then I realize, oh no, I forgot my outline.

03:00.900 --> 03:04.230
So we can use that amend option that I showed you.

03:04.230 --> 03:07.320
We need to add this file, so we're going to stage it.

03:07.320 --> 03:09.900
Again, this only works if I haven't made another commit.

03:09.900 --> 03:10.733
I can't go back

03:10.733 --> 03:13.440
and edit a commit from, you know, 10 commits ago,

03:13.440 --> 03:16.260
but I did just make that commit and I'm going to amend it.

03:16.260 --> 03:20.880
So I'll add that file, but I'm not gonna commit like normal.

03:20.880 --> 03:24.960
I'm gonna do git commit --amend.

03:24.960 --> 03:25.793
All right?

03:25.793 --> 03:28.710
And this is going to open up in VS Code

03:28.710 --> 03:31.500
because that's what I've configured my editor to be

03:31.500 --> 03:34.440
and it opens up the previous commit message

03:34.440 --> 03:36.750
and this allows me to edit that commit message.

03:36.750 --> 03:40.800
So if I had a typo in here, I could change it.

03:40.800 --> 03:42.510
I could, you know, if I misspelled,

03:42.510 --> 03:45.090
sometimes that's the reason for amending.

03:45.090 --> 03:47.250
You might not stage any new files.

03:47.250 --> 03:49.080
You might just wanna fix a commit message.

03:49.080 --> 03:51.270
Just git commit --amend.

03:51.270 --> 03:54.240
That will open up in whatever editor you've used.

03:54.240 --> 03:56.880
Hopefully you've configured it so that it's not Vim,

03:56.880 --> 03:58.923
or hopefully you know how to use Vim.

03:59.880 --> 04:01.350
So I have VS Code to go back

04:01.350 --> 04:03.480
and watch a video where I did that if you need it

04:03.480 --> 04:06.000
and now I can change the message if needed,

04:06.000 --> 04:08.670
but if I don't wanna change the message, I wanna leave it,

04:08.670 --> 04:10.140
which I do

04:10.140 --> 04:13.590
and I just want to include that new files or files.

04:13.590 --> 04:15.510
In this case, it's just the outline.

04:15.510 --> 04:17.910
All I need to do is save this file and close it.

04:18.810 --> 04:21.150
So I'm not gonna change the commit at all.

04:21.150 --> 04:25.080
You can see it's also put modified outline.txt in here.

04:25.080 --> 04:27.450
That's now going to be included.

04:27.450 --> 04:30.360
I'll close outta there and there we are.

04:30.360 --> 04:33.060
Four files changed, that's what it said in that commit.

04:33.060 --> 04:37.260
Previously it was just three, but now I amended that commit.

04:37.260 --> 04:41.070
I basically redid it and I included the outline.

04:41.070 --> 04:43.230
All right, so there's two reasons to do this.

04:43.230 --> 04:46.950
One is to include files changes that you forgot,

04:46.950 --> 04:50.070
that you somehow messed up in the previous commit.

04:50.070 --> 04:52.800
The other option is just to redo the commit message,

04:52.800 --> 04:54.840
or you could do both of them at the same time.

04:54.840 --> 04:56.730
You just need to add --amend

04:56.730 --> 04:59.733
and that allows you to redo the previous commit.
